Ah—there it is.

Not “you know me.”

You’ve heard of me.

That’s a different kind of presence entirely: reputation arriving before the body does. Rumor, story, consequence, fragments of other people’s accounts assembling a silhouette in advance.

Very Jack Sparrow energy:

“But you have heard of me.”

And in romanticized-life mode, that becomes a useful principle: leave enough wake that the world carries some part of your story ahead of you.